# Pawpaw: The Fastest Fruit From Seed to Harvest

Pawpaw is the sprint of the fruit world: seed to first harvest in **8–12 months**, bearing year-round for 3–5 years. No other fruit crop pays this fast — which is why pawpaw is the classic "plant it while the orchard grows" crop that funds an avocado or mango investment. The plan has two traps worth knowing before planting: males that never fruit, and a virus that kills everything.

## Where pawpaw grows well

- **Altitude:** 0–1,800 m
- **Rainfall:** 1,000–1,500 mm, or irrigation
- **Temperature:** 22–32 °C — pawpaw hates cold and hates wet feet
- **Soil:** deep, fertile, well-drained loam, pH 6.0–7.0

## Pick the right variety

**Sunrise Solo** — the export standard: small 500–800 g fruit, sweet red flesh, the variety buyers in Middle East markets ask for. **Mountain pawpaw** — the local workhorse: bigger fruit, tough, open-market favourite. **Calina F1** — hybrid vigour and ringspot tolerance, at hybrid seed cost.

Seed is cheap and the crop is fast: sow **2–3 seeds per hole** at **2.5 m × 2.5 m** (roughly 640 plants per acre), or raise in a nursery and transplant at 8 weeks.

## The timeline that matters

- **Week 0 (sowing):** sow fresh seed 1 cm deep — pawpaw seed germinates in 2–3 weeks when fresh. Mix well-rotted manure into the holes.
- **Week 8:** thin to the strongest seedling per hole.
- **Month 3 (the male cull):** when the first flowers open, identify and remove the **male plants** — long flower stalks with many small flowers and no fruit. Keep females and hermaphrodites (self-fertile, best fruit). Left unchecked, males become half your stand and produce nothing.
- **Month 3–12:** top-dress **manure + NPK per plant every 2 months** — pawpaw is a heavy, continuous feeder.
- **Month 8–12 (first harvest):** first fruit colours and softens at the tip. Pick at the first colour break; pawpaw ripens off the plant.
- **Year 2–5:** the plant bears continuously; yields run **30–60 kg per plant per year**.
- **Replanting rhythm:** pawpaw declines after 4–5 years — keep a nursery strip going so the replacement cycle never stops.

Panda's season planner tracks the male cull, the feeding rounds, and the replanting strip — in English and Swahili.

## Pests and diseases that cost pawpaw farmers money

1. **Papaya ringspot virus (PRSV)** — the industry killer: mottled, distorted leaves, oily ring spots on fruit, plants stop producing and die back. No cure — rogue infected plants immediately, control aphids early, and plant tolerant varieties (Calina).
2. **Powdery mildew** — white dusting on leaves in warm-dry spells; sulphur-class products at first dusting.
3. **Anthracnose** — sunken spots on ripening fruit; registered anthracnose-active sprays and clean handling.
4. **Damping off / root rot** — seedling collapse and dying plants in wet soil; raised beds and strict drainage — pawpaw roots drown easily.
5. **Fruit fly** — stings on ripening fruit; registered bait sprays and remove fallen fruit.

Advisory note: Panda's treatment guidance is advisory — always use PCPB-registered products at label rates and observe pre-harvest intervals. When in doubt, your local extension officer sees the same photo you do.

## Year-round fruit, two markets

Local pawpaw sells at **20–50 KES per kg** through the year with little seasonality — a fruiting plant is a continuous trickle of income. Export-grade Solo pays several multiples but demands grading discipline and exporter contracts, same as avocado. The smallholder strategy: Solo for the exporter, Mountain for the local market, one strip replanted every year so the flow never stops.

## The bottom line

Establishment: **20,000–35,000 KES per acre**. First revenue: month 8–12 — the fastest fruit return in this catalogue. Pawpaw is the cash crop you plant while the permanent orchard grows: it pays the land rent in year one.

